WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.837 --> 00:00:14.321
Living a life of luxury and comfort was very agreeable to Miss Amy March.

2
00:00:15.321 --> 00:00:21.025
Italy was also very agreeable to Amy, who found it even more beautiful and romantic than she imagined.

3
00:00:22.006 --> 00:00:26.569
Every afternoon, she explored the villages near the one where she and Aunt March were staying.

4
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:33.037
Her sketchbook and paints were her constant companion in case she was inspired to capture a beautiful scene.

5
00:00:34.358 --> 00:00:40.040
As in France, Amy drew a lot of attention from passersby and even though she didn't speak their

6
00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:44.882
language, her generous smile and friendly tone of voice were universally understood.

7
00:00:45.762 --> 00:00:50.824
Yet as time wore on, Amy began to grow restless and wonder if perhaps there was something more

8
00:00:50.864 --> 00:00:53.765
to life than just being content with material things.

9
00:00:54.706 --> 00:01:00.973
She also became very homesick, wanting more than anything to see a familiar face, talk in her native

10
00:01:01.013 --> 00:01:03.475
language, and walk down a well-known road.

11
00:01:04.336 --> 00:01:09.100
She kept in touch with her family and friends through letters, but it was not the same as

12
00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:10.521
being with those she loved.

13
00:01:11.642 --> 00:01:17.527
One day, finding an isolated shady spot under a large tree, Amy began to paint the charming local

14
00:01:17.607 --> 00:01:19.368
children playing a game in the field.

15
00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:26.054
Smiling to herself, she was reminded of how she used to play outdoors with her sisters at that

16
00:01:26.154 --> 00:01:30.477
age. Unknown to Amy, an old acquaintance was very close by.

17
00:01:31.277 --> 00:01:35.999
He, too, was unaware that, in trying to get away from one March sister, he was soon to

18
00:01:36.059 --> 00:01:37.000
encounter another one.

19
00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:42.602
Laurie thought, or rather hoped, that a trip to Europe would cure him of his broken heart.

20
00:01:43.342 --> 00:01:48.864
He visited old friends, went to his favorite restaurants, and attended social events where he met many likeable

21
00:01:48.924 --> 00:01:53.006
young ladies, but he found that no one could ever compare with his Jo.

22
00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:57.348
So he decided to give in to his tormented heart and his self-pity.

23
00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:03.403
Wearing a long face (not to mention an equally long moustache and beard since he had given up

24
00:02:03.503 --> 00:02:07.425
on shaving as well), Laurie walked the streets of Rome alone and depressed.

25
00:02:09.546 --> 00:02:15.009
Jo, on the other hand, had put aside her own sorrow over Beth's tragedy to focus on how

26
00:02:15.069 --> 00:02:17.891
she could make Beth's last days more meaningful and fulfilling.

27
00:02:19.151 --> 00:02:25.175
One day, when the two sisters were sitting outside in the yard, Beth had said dreamily, "I've never

28
00:02:25.275 --> 00:02:27.276
seen buffalo except in paintings.

29
00:02:28.064 --> 00:02:31.885
They are such majestic creatures, yet they seem so gentle.

30
00:02:32.845 --> 00:02:35.386
It's a shame they don't roam this part of the country."

31
00:02:36.826 --> 00:02:38.307
"Would you like to see buffalo?"

32
00:02:38.587 --> 00:02:44.368
Jo asked, a little surprised at her sister's interest in such a wild animal and not in something

33
00:02:44.428 --> 00:02:46.109
milder and tamer like a giraffe.

34
00:02:46.909 --> 00:02:52.951
Beth smiled shyly, saying, "They live out west so it isn't likely that I'll ever see one, as

35
00:02:53.111 --> 00:02:54.171
much as I'd like to."

36
00:02:55.411 --> 00:03:00.609
This conversation gave Jo an idea but she didn't tell Beth because she wanted it to be a

37
00:03:00.649 --> 00:03:07.671
surprise. Knowing that the Eagle would buy her stories (if the editor liked them, of course), Jo decided

38
00:03:07.711 --> 00:03:09.751
to consider different publishers as well.

39
00:03:10.572 --> 00:03:14.733
She had a few completed stories along with the couple she was working on at the moment.

40
00:03:15.893 --> 00:03:21.214
"If I write three or four stories a week," she calculated, "then I could save enough money to

41
00:03:21.254 --> 00:03:23.835
take Beth to California, where she can see buffalo."

42
00:03:24.995 --> 00:03:28.697
With that plan as an incentive, Jo spared no time for herself.

43
00:03:29.417 --> 00:03:34.420
Every free minute that she had was spent writing, then taking her stories to the publishers in town.

44
00:03:35.401 --> 00:03:40.864
Physically and mentally exhausted, Jo kept herself going by keeping Beth's wish close to her heart.

45
00:03:41.684 --> 00:03:47.447
On an exceptionally glorious day in Rome, Amy, equipped with her sketchbook and pencils, strolled down a quiet

46
00:03:47.507 --> 00:03:49.648
road, looking for a place to sit and have coffee.

47
00:03:50.609 --> 00:03:55.351
Her eye caught sight of two little children standing in front of a bakery eating ice cream on

48
00:03:55.371 --> 00:03:56.432
the other side of the road.

49
00:03:57.108 --> 00:04:02.012
"I think I'll have one of those," she said aloud, then crossed over to the store the children

50
00:04:02.052 --> 00:04:02.733
had just left.

51
00:04:03.634 --> 00:04:09.098
She noticed a man sitting at a table by himself, sipping coffee, with a raisin bun left untouched

52
00:04:09.178 --> 00:04:09.679
on his plate.

53
00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:15.164
At first glance he seemed shabby and dirty, so Amy quickly looked away.

54
00:04:16.044 --> 00:04:21.028
As she waited for the baker to scoop out her ice cream, however, Amy stole another glance because

55
00:04:21.069 --> 00:04:23.130
there was something oddly familiar about him.

56
00:04:23.931 --> 00:04:29.239
Thanking the baker, Amy paid for her ice cream and deliberately walked past the silent man, who did

57
00:04:29.279 --> 00:04:30.901
not even look up as she went by.

58
00:04:32.382 --> 00:04:35.905
When it suddenly dawned on her, Amy swung around in disbelief.

59
00:04:35.925 --> 00:04:38.887
"Laurie? Is that you?"

60
00:04:39.327 --> 00:04:40.288
she whispered loudly.

61
00:04:41.069 --> 00:04:44.792
The man slowly lifted his eyes, which Amy noted were red and dull.

62
00:04:45.653 --> 00:04:50.757
Then, recognizing the young woman standing before him, he broke out into a big, wide grin.

63
00:04:50.777 --> 00:04:52.958
"Little Amy March?

64
00:04:53.539 --> 00:04:54.560
I must be dreaming!"

65
00:04:55.008 --> 00:04:58.832
he yelled, knocking aside his bun and nearly upsetting his coffee in the process.

66
00:04:58.852 --> 00:05:02.436
"Laurie? I hardly recognized you!"

67
00:05:02.716 --> 00:05:05.899
Amy said, trying hard not to stare at his strange appearance.

68
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:08.001
"Look who's talking!

69
00:05:08.401 --> 00:05:14.407
You dress like a European now, and your hair Laurie complimented her, in a voice and manner more

70
00:05:14.467 --> 00:05:15.729
like the old Laurie she knew.

71
00:05:15.749 --> 00:05:17.170
"Oh, Laurie!

72
00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:18.511
I can't believe you're here.

73
00:05:19.356 --> 00:05:24.098
I was getting so homesick and I thought about leaving Aunt March and returning home just to see

74
00:05:24.138 --> 00:05:26.099
a familiar face," cried Amy.

75
00:05:26.119 --> 00:05:28.220
"Well, I don't blame you.

76
00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.281
You've been away for almost a year now," Laurie said.

77
00:05:31.301 --> 00:05:34.082
"Now that I'm here, we have a lot to catch up on."

78
00:05:34.102 --> 00:05:35.943
"I would really like that.

79
00:05:36.423 --> 00:05:43.986
Tell me everything about home: Meg's baby, Jo's New York excursion," Amy stopped when she noticed Laurie's face

80
00:05:44.066 --> 00:05:44.707
harden again.

81
00:05:44.727 --> 00:05:47.368
"But we'll leave all that for later.

82
00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:51.621
First of all, let's go for a walk," she said quickly to change the subject.

83
00:05:52.281 --> 00:05:55.902
And taking his arm, Amy chatted away as they walked the streets of Rome.

84
00:05:57.122 --> 00:06:03.324
That evening, Aunt March noticed a change in Amy's spirits as the young lady sang softly and smiled

85
00:06:03.384 --> 00:06:06.205
a great deal while getting ready to go out to a party.

86
00:06:06.225 --> 00:06:09.166
"What put that smile on your face?"

87
00:06:09.566 --> 00:06:11.526
Aunt March said in her usual gruff manner.

88
00:06:11.546 --> 00:06:16.948
"You'll never believe who I ran into on the street today, Aunt," Amy said, accustomed to her aunt's

89
00:06:16.988 --> 00:06:17.768
manner by now.

90
00:06:18.948 --> 00:06:23.750
"I hope it was young Fred and you begged him to forgive you for your foolish mistake of

91
00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:30.153
saying no to his proposal," the old lady snapped, still angry about Amy's refusal to marry him.

92
00:06:30.993 --> 00:06:35.015
Amy brushed aside her aunt's remark and said cheerily, "I ran into Laurie!

93
00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:36.575
What a coincidence!

94
00:06:37.716 --> 00:06:41.637
He's here for a while so we decided to catch up and see some sights together."

95
00:06:41.657 --> 00:06:47.760
"Humph," grunted Aunt March, thinking that Laurie wasn't a bad sort but she still preferred Fred.

96
00:06:48.436 --> 00:06:50.837
Shortly after, the servant announced Mr.

97
00:06:50.877 --> 00:06:51.838
Laurence's arrival.

98
00:06:52.818 --> 00:06:57.820
To Amy's immense relief, Laurie had shaven off his moustache and beard and looked very handsome in his

99
00:06:57.880 --> 00:06:58.560
coat and tie.

100
00:06:59.441 --> 00:07:04.523
After he had greeted Aunt March, he and Amy went out, laughing and talking, both of them delighted

101
00:07:04.583 --> 00:07:06.584
and comforted to be with an old friend.
